e we're having  some trouble with uh julia hans line  there  julia can you hear us i'll try one more  time here  i can hear you now okay julia we were  just taking a look at your report and i  wanted to know if you  could explain to us why there are  different restrictions in turkey for the  young  and for the old  well the turkish government first  introduced stay-at-home orders for those  aged 65  and older and those aged 20 or younger  in april when the pandemic started  basically arguing that the elderly are a  risk group  that needs protection while asymptomatic  young people  might fuel the spread of the virus now  both age groups combined make up about  40 of the turkish population and since  most of them are not part of the  workforce keeping them at home and  that's certainly part of the reasoning  here  is less harmful to the economy than  let's say similar measures targeted at  the working  population in spring though the curfews  were much  tougher the young and the elderly were  completely banned from leaving their  homes for about two months  and the authorities argued that this  helped bring the numbers down so what  we're seeing now  is somehow a softer version of the  curfews we saw earlier this year  while the overall coronavirus situation  here in turkey is much worse  than it was in spring you mentioned in  your report you leave  the discrepancies between how turkey has  been reporting new covet 19  infections so how clear is the picture  there of the situation right now  well my impression is that the pandemic  here in turkey  risks spiraling out of control many  people we speak to  either know someone who tested positive  for covet 19 or  someone who even died from the virus now  we have the official figures published  by the turkish health ministry  and according to this we had more than  33  000 new infections in the last 24 hours  that's the highest number reported since  the beginning of the pandemic it's also  the highest number in  europe